." 
  <h3 align=center>Explanation of labels, label sets, and lists of label sets assigned to a file.</h3>

  <p>When some are lost, can't find there way... Some find labels can save the day :-)</p>

  <p>Around each corner, hoping to see, a way to read a disassembly, a way to break free from bland addresses.</p>

  <p>
    Ok, enough Alternate Reality jokes;=) Basically the disassembler looks up the label addresses 
    in the list of label sets assigned to the file and shows the text. That's it. 
    I chose to organize the labels into sets, and assign those sets to files for 3 reasons:
    <ul>
      <li>So the disassembler wouldn't have to search through all the labels every time, speeding it up.</li>
      <li>So you wouldn't see text for a label for another file that happens to have the same address.</li>
      <li>And so the same label wouldn't have to be duplicated in multiple sets.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    To get this scheme to work some rules need to be followed which are:
    <ul>
      <li>Make a label set for each game file, and one for the atari operating system rom.</li>
      <li>For game code addresses, assign labels to the file the code is in.</li>
      <li>For operating system code addresses, assign the labels to the atarios label set.</li>
      <li>For game variables, try assigning the labels the the game file that first uses them.</li>
      <li>For operating system variables, assign the labels to the atarios label set.</li>
      <li>Assign label sets to files according to which game files are in memory at the same time.</li>
      <li>Every list of label sets will likely include the atarios set because it is always in memory
           unless the game file it is for does not use the operating system.</li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <p>
    For example, when the City disk 1 side 1 is first booted, the only things in memory are the operating system, and 
    the CityBoot.xfd game file. So the label sets assigned to it are atarios and cityboot. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the CityBoot.xfd code loads the CityTitleLoader.xfd game file and runs the CityTitleLoader.xfd's code.
    CityTitleLoader.xfd uses some of CityBoot.xfd's code (I think), so the label sets assigned to
    CityTitleLoader.xfd are atarios, cityboot, and citytitleloader.
  </p>
"

drawprojectfooter